// The dungeon — the first level that uses the whole kit at once: fire on a
// cycle, a knight to fight, a spiked pit crossed on a moving platform, a
// gallery of one-way plank over the last hall, and a second knight that is
// worth thinking about rather than walking into.
//
// Legend: '#' solid  '=' one-way platform  '^' spikes  'F' fire (house cycle)
//         'P' spawn  'K' knight.  The moving platform and the second fire have
// no grid character — a character can say where a thing is but not where it
// goes, or when — so they are entries in the list below.
//
// 68x13 tiles at 32px = 2176x416 world pixels. The floor is row 10 the whole
// way across, surface y=320, except where the pit is cut out of it.
//
//   spawn        col 2,  row 9    world (70, 286)
//   fire A       col 10, row 9    grid F, so the house cycle: lit ticks 0-59
//                of every 120, out 60-119. Box x 326..346.
//   fire B       col 13, row 9    authored at phase 60, the exact opposite of
//                A. Box x 422..442.
//   rubble       col 18 and col 29, row 9 — one-tile steps
//   knight 0     col 23, row 9    penned between the two rubble steps
//   pit          cols 34-45, spikes on row 11 — x 1088..1472, 384px
//   gangways     col 34 and col 45, row 10 — one-way plank, surface y=320
//   platform     3 tiles solid, top y=320, level with both lips and both
//                gangways
//   landing      cols 46-51
//   stair        cols 48-51, row 8, one-way — surface y=256, its right
//                edge flush under the gallery's left one
//   gallery      cols 52-66, row 6, one-way — surface y=192
//   rubble       col 52, row 9 — the last hall's threshold
//   knight 1     col 57, row 9    paces x 1702..2057, penned by that step at
//                one end and the stairwell's lip at the other
//   stairwell    cols 65-66, a step down out of row 10 — floor y=352; the way
//                deeper, and the end of the run
//
// Every pen in this map is a **32px step**, which is a wall to anything that
// walks and nothing at all to anything that jumps. It is what keeps each fight
// in the room it was designed for without putting a door in the player's way,
// and the stairwell's lip does the same job with a step *down*: a patroller
// turns at the edge of what it is standing on, so nothing follows you out.
//
// ## The fire is a two-beat crossing, not a sprint
//
// A and B alternate, and they are three tiles apart — so the gap that lets you
// past A has closed by the time you reach B. Running straight through kills
// you at B around tick 114. The crossing is: go when A goes out, stop on the
// clear tile between them (x 346..422 is inside neither box), and go again
// when B goes out. `tapes/dungeon_run.tape` walks exactly that and survives
// it; the same tape without the pause is a death.
//
// ## The pit is what gates the back half
//
// 384px is not jumpable. A standing jump carries about 148px and a jump plus
// the air jump about 283px, so the platform is the only way over and the
// spikes are what happens if you disagree. The path is level with both lips so
// the ride is walked onto and off rather than leapt between.
//
// It does **not** dock flush against them. A platform that comes all the way
// to a wall shuts on anything standing in front of it, with nowhere legal for
// the physics to put a body — the clearance rule in `tests/levels.rs` has the
// argument, and this map used to break it at both ends. So the path stops a
// tile short each way (x 1120, and a right edge of 1440) and the last 32px is
// a one-way plank: a floor without a wall, which carries the walk-on ride
// across the clearance without giving the ferry anything to pin you against.
//
// ## Why the second knight is a different problem from the first
//
// `Hostile` sight is a **box in front**, not a radius:
// `npc::sighted` rejects a target more than `sight` (130px) ahead, behind the
// facing at all, or more than `sight_height` (36px) off the eyeline. All three
// halves of that are used here.
//
// *Knight 0* is a fair fight and is meant to be. It is penned in an open room
// with a rubble step at each end, on the player's own floor, so walking in
// puts you inside its sight box facing it — 130px of warning, one swing each
// way, and nowhere for either of you to be clever. That is the head-on fight
// the level teaches first, because the second one only reads as a lesson if
// you have already had the honest version.
//
// *Knight 1* is the same knight in a room with a roof over it. The gallery on
// row 6 is 128px above the hall floor, so a player walking it is 130px off the
// eyeline — outside `sight_height` by a factor of three, whatever the knight
// is facing and however close it is underneath. You can therefore walk the
// whole length of its patrol, watch it, and pick where to come down; and
// because the gallery is one-way plank rather than stone, coming down is
// `down` on any tile of it rather than a fixed hole the map chose for you.
// Drop while it is walking away and you close 130px of its sight box for free
// and open the fight with a hit instead of taking one. Drop in front of it and
// you get knight 0 again, minus the room to back off.
//
// The head-on route into the same hall is still there — over the rubble at
// col 52, at floor level, into whatever is facing you — so this is a choice
// the map offers rather than a route it forces, which is the only way a rule
// like the sight box is ever actually learned.
//
// ## What is at the end
//
// Knight 1 guards the stair down, and what it is carrying is the reward for
// dealing with it: its loot table (assets/data/stats.ron) is the only source
// of items in the game outside a conversation, so the treasure at the bottom
// of the dungeon is the thing that was holding it. Maps cannot place a pickup
// today — there is no `Item(...)` entity form — so this is the whole of what
// "the item at the end" can mean without a code change.
Level(
    tileset: "castle",
    grid: [
        "####################################################################",
        "#..................................................................#",
        "#..................................................................#",
        "#..................................................................#",
        "#..................................................................#",
        "#..................................................................#",
        "#...................................................===============#",
        "#..................................................................#",
        "#...............................................====...............#",
        "#.P.......F.......#....K.....#......................#....K.........#",
        "##################################=..........=###################..#",
        "##################################^^^^^^^^^^^^######################",
        "####################################################################",
    ],
    entities: [
        // A's opposite number: same cycle, half a period out of step, so the
        // pair are never out at the same time and the corridor has a rhythm
        // rather than a gap.
        Fire(cell: (13, 9), phase: 60),
        // The ferry. A tile of clearance at each end leaves 224px of travel;
        // at 75 px/s that is 179 ticks a leg and 358 a round trip, so the
        // crossing still takes the three seconds the level was tuned around.
        // The phase puts it back against the near gangway on ticks 442, 800,
        // 1158, …; 800 is a few ticks after a player who has fought knight 0
        // and walked over arrives, so the ride is waited for and then boarded
        // rather than jumped at.
        Platform(from: (35, 10), to: (42, 10), tiles: 3, speed: 75.0, phase: 274),
    ],
)
